    bpf: get rid of 1-page vmalloc allocations
    =

    I've noticed a bunch of 1-page big vmalloc() allocations,
    created by the bpf code:
    =

      0x000000008f14d715-0x000000001d6f4e9c    8192 bpf_prog_alloc+0x47/0xd=
0 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
      0x0000000042422afe-0x00000000209031b2    8192 bpf_prog_alloc+0x47/0xd=
0 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
      0x00000000d67b5c52-0x00000000df2adbf9    8192 bpf_prog_alloc+0x47/0xd=
0 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
      0x000000008dc72ab6-0x00000000ba190c6a    8192 bpf_jit_binary_alloc+0x=
32/0x90 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
      0x00000000ba190c6a-0x0000000091903153    8192 bpf_jit_binary_alloc+0x=
32/0x90 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
      0x0000000091903153-0x00000000f0675c04    8192 bpf_jit_binary_alloc+0x=
32/0x90 pages=3D1 vmalloc N0=3D1
    =

      $ cat /proc/vmallocinfo | grep bpf | grep "pages=3D1" | wc -l
      362
    =

    It's horrible inefficient both in terms of allocation/freeing speed
    and memory waste (single vmalloc() call will lead to at least 3
    metadata allocations).
    =

    Let's replace the remaining vmalloc()s in bpf code with kvmalloc(),
    which falls back to simple kmalloc() for small allocations.
    =

    Signed-off-by: Roman Gushchin <guro@fb.com>
    Cc: Daniel Borkmann <daniel@iogearbox.net>
    Cc: Alexei Starovoitov <ast@kernel.org>

